<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    The storage ladder (renewal lane 2, docs/design/era-progression.md 7.11): one form of the settlement's store
    for each era after the first, as the dwelling ladder has one house per era. StorageHut (Buildings_Structural.xml)
    is the first rung and stays as it was. Each form fills the same need (Building.StorageForms, counted with the hut
    by StoneWallMaterials.EquivalentsOf) and replaces a standing store in place when the town renews it
    (Building.StructureRenewal). Like the hut each is one cell, impassable and roofed: the goods themselves are in the
    ledger, and a store on the map is the town's capacity to keep them.

    Made from stuff, as RimWorld's buildings are: the form is the era's, the material is what the town holds (a
    bulk metal, then cut stone, then wood, which a builder can always fell), so every era's store can be built from
    what a town actually produces. Gates, sizes and costs are this port's own; RimWorld has no storage building
    but the shelf (recalled). The labels are the real stores of each age, not RimWorld's.
  -->

  <ThingDef>
    <defName>Granary</defName>
    <label>granary</label>
    <description>A raised, roofed store on posts: dry grain and goods kept off the damp ground and out of reach of vermin.</description>
    <category>Building</category>
    <thingClass>SimWorld.Building.Building</thingClass>
    <passability>Impassable</passability>
    <fillPercent>0</fillPercent>
    <statBases>
      <MaxHitPoints>190</MaxHitPoints>
      <WorkToBuild>420</WorkToBuild>
    </statBases>
    <researchPrerequisites>
      <li>Granaries</li>
    </researchPrerequisites>
    <stuffCategories>
      <li>Metallic</li>
      <li>Woody</li>
      <li>Stony</li>
    </stuffCategories>
    <costStuffCount>30</costStuffCount>
  </ThingDef>

